From d907f86a30869e29eca2eb874991843452880921 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 18:27:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(api): public computation API v1, plus
 documentation-comment cleanup

Exposes the deterministic engine under an API key so a Stadtwerk, EPC or
platform can run qualification inside their own process.

Endpoints (all keyed; /api/v1 answers 401 entirely unless ET_API_KEYS is set):
  GET  /api/v1/meta        model stamp, every rate table with bands, legal basis
                           and validity window, the three participation models
  POST /api/v1/calculate   three scenarios, optional 20-year schedule
  POST /api/v1/eligibility verdict, findings, missing data, next paid step

Every successful response carries three things a wrapper cannot produce:
  - model      version, assumption set, jurisdiction, currency, timestamp, so a
               result is reproducible and two results are comparable only when
               they actually are
  - provenance per input: source class, verified flag, and whether the caller
               supplied the value or it came from a sourced default
  - freshness  the validity window of each regulated rate used, so an
               integrator can tell whether the answer is still current

Omitted inputs default to the sourced rate FOR THE GIVEN PLANT SIZE, since the
regulated rates are banded. Verified live: an 88 kWp request resolves to
5,44 ct/kWh feed-in and 1,29 ct/kWh Zuschlag (40-100 band), not the 30 kWp
values. A size outside the published schedule returns the default plus an
explicit rate_out_of_published_band warning rather than an extrapolation.

Auth: keys are held as SHA-256 hashes in ET_API_KEYS as label:hash pairs. The
plaintext is issued once by `pnpm apikey:new <label>` and never stored, so a
leaked environment yields no working keys. Comparison is constant-time and every
configured entry is compared even after a match.

Freshness engine (shared/tariffs.ts): each dated table reports current /
expiring / expired / open_ended against its own window, expiredTables() names
any that have lapsed, and a test asserts none ships expired.

The engine moved from client/src/lib/mieterstrom.ts to shared/mieterstrom.ts so
the browser and the API run identical code; the old path re-exports it, so no
client import changed.

Also: documentation comments across the codebase rewritten to describe what the
code does and why, with narrative about prior states removed.

Verified against a running build: 401 without a key, 401 with a wrong key, 400
on invalid input, 200 with provenance and freshness populated; smoke 20/20.

Evidence: pnpm check clean, 217/217 vitest (16 API contract + auth, 7 freshness,
11 rewritten), pnpm build clean.
